WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
The end.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:49.240
About 340 million years ago, a brand-new family of animals was evolving in the primeval swamps.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:54.400
They were to go one step further than the amphibians, who had emerged onto dry land

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:04.600
before them, for they would eventually completely cut their ties with water.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:12.720
They were the ancestors of today's lizards.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:33.040
They evolved scaly, impermeable skins and moved up into the forests.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:44.400
They diversified into a multitude of different shapes and sizes.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:55.480
They developed signalling systems to communicate with one another.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:01.960
And they squabbled, as animals do, over mates and territory.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:12.680
For food, they hunted insects that were already well established on the land, in great numbers.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:28.480
And here, without returning to water, they produced their families.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.920
They powered their bodies not only with food, but with the heat that they drew directly

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.760
from the sun.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:44.960
As they diversified, so they spread into the harshest of the land's habitats, the baking,

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:54.280
wartless deserts, which eventually they would come to dominate.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:59.040
The bigger ones are truly powerful and fearless.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:08.280
Rearing up, they're well able to defend themselves with their front legs if they're threatened.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.520
This is a very intelligent animal.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:19.640
It is observing me, just as I'm observing it.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:27.080
It's a monitor lizard, and it's king of this country, the Australian outback.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:32.560
It is frightened of pretty well nothing, obviously, including me.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.880
And it'll chase and hunt and eat pretty well anything.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:45.640
There are several thousands lizards around the world, and they are truly the dragons

00:03:45.640 --> 00:04:01.320
of the dry.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:09.080
Their eggs on land had to be encased in shells to prevent them from drying out.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:15.760
And what better place to lay them could a mother lizard find than a termite's nest?

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.320
Worker termites labor unceasingly to keep the temperature and humidity virtually constant

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:22.960
for their own benefit.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:33.880
But that also makes their mound a near perfect incubator for eggs of others.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:56.360
After 10 months, they're beginning to hatch.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:10.360
These are baby lace monitors.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.760
But they face a major problem.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:19.000
A termite nest's walls can be a foot thick and extremely hard, too hard for the young

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.100
monitors to break through.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:30.560
They are imprisoned with no food.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.960
For a week after hatching, they're sustained by the last of the yolk that remains in their

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:36.960
stomachs.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:48.080
But when that comes to an end, they could starve.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:59.080
An adult lace monitor is nearby.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:02.320
It may or may not be the baby's mother.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:08.520
If not, then it could be a threat for monitors or hunters and will eat most small animals,

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:16.400
including baby lizards.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:29.320
She's nearing the termite nest within which the young are trapped.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:33.120
She could be looking for a place to lay her eggs.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:52.120
