But it's not his head that impresses his rivals so much, it's the underside, which if he's a high-status male, will be bright orange and yellow.

00:37:03.840 --> 00:37:12.840
And if another one turns up, he will try and impress his rival by exposing that.

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:20.840
These awkward-looking postures reveal why these creatures are called flat lizards.

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:33.840
By regularly displaying their vivid badges, the males repeatedly confirm their place in the pecking order and so keep fighting to a minimum.

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:50.840
As a female moves from one territory to another, so each male courts her in turn.

00:37:50.840 --> 00:38:07.840
And now they're really warmed up and active, and whole groups of them are beginning to travel down across the rocks towards the river where they'll find their food.

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:16.840
But down here where the flies swarm, it's a free-for-all, and that causes a lot of trouble.

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:29.840
Catching flies is necessarily an acrobatic business.

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:46.840
But you can't leap for flies and still keep properly spaced out, so there are inevitably quarrels between rival males.

00:38:46.840 --> 00:38:52.840
Females, on the other hand, are only interested in getting a good meal.

00:38:52.840 --> 00:38:57.840
Fired-up males, however, have other ideas.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:12.840
For them, there is more to life than just dinner.

00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:28.840
Some won't take no for an answer.

00:39:28.840 --> 00:39:36.840
The females want food.

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:47.840
They need a square meal to nourish the eggs that are developing within them.

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:56.840
But they won't get any peace until they leave the restaurant and get back home, where life is better regulated.

00:39:56.840 --> 00:40:06.840
The high-octane social life of the flat lizards with its constant squabbling seems to be very stressful.

00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:31.840
It's less frequent, but altogether more impressive.

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:34.840
A Mexican beaded lizard.

00:40:34.840 --> 00:40:42.840
One of the few lizards in the world with a poisonous bite, and a very virulent one it is, too.

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:55.840
In the spring, rival males fight according to a very specific set of rules.

00:40:55.840 --> 00:41:19.840
They use neither their sharp, powerful claws nor their poisonous bite in their battles.

00:41:19.840 --> 00:41:33.840
At first, they grapple rather warily to assess each other's strength.

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:53.840
Then they begin to wrestle in earnest, each trying to pin down the other on the ground.

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:57.840
These two are evenly matched. Neither can get the crucial throw.

00:41:57.840 --> 00:42:25.840
It's rather like an arm wrestling contest, and the bout can continue for several hours.

00:42:25.840 --> 00:42:45.840
The eventual winner is the one who ends up on top most frequently.

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:56.840
It's a controlled test of strength in which, despite their lethal weaponry, no one gets seriously hurt.

00:42:56.840 --> 00:43:03.840
Other lizards defend themselves not with physical strength, but by deceit.

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:06.840
The South African desert.

00:43:06.840 --> 00:43:12.840
A bushveld lizard.

00:43:12.840 --> 00:43:20.840
This is another. It looks very different, but that is because it's a baby.

00:43:20.840 --> 00:43:37.840
It not only has different coloration, it also walks in a very different and quite extraordinary way.

00:43:37.840 --> 00:43:49.840
It appears to be imitating one of the local beetles, that one, and to discover why, I'm going to take defensive measures with these goggles.

00:43:49.840 --> 00:43:58.840
This beetle is known as an uchpister, an eye-spitter. That's because it's squirting formic acid at me.

00:43:58.840 --> 00:44:14.840
Yeah, and if any of that got into my eye, it would be extremely painful. It's a defensive system, and the lizards are benefiting by imitating a beetle with that kind of armory.

00:44:14.840 --> 00:44:34.840
The young lizard closely matches the beetle, both in its appearance and its walk, so birds that prey on lizards assume it has a nasty spray and leave it alone.

00:44:34.840 --> 00:44:49.840
Lizards can cope with dry, hot conditions so well that they dominate the fauna in tropical deserts around the world, including those in central Australia.

00:44:49.840 --> 00:45:08.840
Their tough, scaly skins prevent their bodies from losing moisture so that they can flourish in these arid, baking-hot lands that other animals find so testing.

00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:20.840
Some wear the most elaborate suits of armour.

00:45:20.840 --> 00:45:41.840
This is surely the most enchanting of lizards. It's called the thorny devil, or Moloch. After Moloch, the god in the Bible who ate little children, both names surely are a slander on such an engaging little animal.

00:45:41.840 --> 00:45:57.840
It feeds entirely on ants, and as you can see, there's not much of a meal in any one of them. But the good thing about ants, as far as Moloch is concerned, is that there's always some around.

00:45:57.840 --> 00:46:12.840
And this little creature will sit by an ant trail patiently, for hours on end, simply picking off one ant at a time.

00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:31.840
The Australian desert is also home to one of the most powerful of the family. Monitors are the kings of lizards, and this is the parenti, the biggest species of monitor in Australia.

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:51.840
It can grow up to two metres long, six feet, and it's a highly intelligent animal. It's got very acute senses of sight and hearing and taste and smell, and like all monitors, it can do something no other kind of lizard can do.

00:46:51.840 --> 00:47:07.840
It can run continuously for a very long time, and that enables it to become an endurance hunter, chasing down its prey.

00:47:07.840 --> 00:47:22.840
Most lizards inflate their lungs using the same muscles they use for walking, so they can't run and breathe effectively at the same time.

00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:39.840
But monitors have big muscular throats, which they use like bellows to pump air into their lungs, and they can do that even when they're running.

00:47:39.840 --> 00:48:00.840
This special way of breathing enables them to reach speeds of over 20 miles an hour. Over distance, they are one of the fastest of all reptiles. The cold-blooded parenti can even outrun a warm-blooded rabbit.

00:48:00.840 --> 00:48:28.840
So the lizards have colonised the world, from swamps to rainforests, from woodland to desert, and in doing so, they've revealed such a variety of form and behaviour that they truly can be called the dragons of the dry.

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:46.840
Much of our filming for this programme was done in Australia. There, there are lizards everywhere. Just walk around in the bush and you'll see them. But usually you won't get much more than a brief glimpse.

00:48:46.840 --> 00:48:54.840
To film their intimate behaviour, we needed help from experts.

00:48:54.840 --> 00:49:03.840
We had travelled to Australia to meet an expert called Mike Bull. He knows Australian lizards as well as anyone.

00:49:03.840 --> 00:49:21.840
He and his team study many species in one small area north of Adelaide, using all manner of gadgets and gizmos to investigate every part of their lives.

00:49:21.840 --> 00:49:36.840
We were particularly interested in the lizards that Mike understands best of all, the shingleback or sleepy lizard. He knows 10,000 of them individually.

00:49:36.840 --> 00:49:53.840
To the faith of it, the sleepy lizard doesn't seem to do a lot, but Mike knows so much about them that we were able to make them one of the stars of our film. He's discovered that they're the only lizards in the world that remain faithful to one partner for all their lives.

00:49:53.840 --> 00:49:56.840
But that wasn't the reason that he began to study them.

00:49:56.840 --> 00:50:01.840
Tell me first how you first saw sleepy lizards and what attracted you to them.

00:50:01.840 --> 00:50:11.840
I first started because I was interested in parasites that live on the lizard. To find the parasites I actually had to look at the lizards as well and I discovered they were doing things that were more interesting than the parasites.

00:50:11.840 --> 00:50:22.840
For me, I think they're one of the most handsome animals that you'll ever find. The other thing is that it's probably the only animal that you know if you're driving along in a car and you see one 100 metres down the road you know you've caught it.

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:30.840
And it's also one that I think I'm going to be sufficiently agile to keep on catching until I'm well past 80.

00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:36.840
Even I, I think, could scrag the sleepy lizard. I'll see what I can manage.

00:50:36.840 --> 00:50:44.840
Sleepy lizards like to bask on warm roads so they're easy to find and they move so slowly they're easy to pick up.

00:50:44.840 --> 00:50:55.840
So the team were able to weigh and measure a whole population and thus discovered that pairs remained together in a way that was previously known only in birds and mammals.

00:50:55.840 --> 00:51:05.840
But that was just the start. Next they turned to technology, some of it advanced, some a little bizarre.

00:51:05.840 --> 00:51:12.840
They used remotely controlled rubber sleepy lizards to test how lizards reacted to one another.

00:51:12.840 --> 00:51:19.840
In this case, not very much.

00:51:19.840 --> 00:51:26.840
Mike's team suspected that another lizard in the area, the giddy skink, had an even more complex social life.

00:51:26.840 --> 00:51:37.840
But this was difficult to prove because when approached the skinks wedged themselves in cracks in the rocks making it impossible to identify who's who.

00:51:37.840 --> 00:51:50.840
The solution was to microchip each lizard so it could then be scanned, just like your supermarket shopping, with a barcode reader on the end of a pole.

00:51:50.840 --> 00:52:04.840
This clever use of technology revealed what looked like a jumble of lizards on a pile of rocks to be actually a little lizard family with young that stay with their parents for life.

00:52:04.840 --> 00:52:13.840
I'm sure that there are going to be many other complex social organizations that will be uncovered in those species if we just simply take the time to look at them.

00:52:13.840 --> 00:52:26.840
But it's just the time and the patience to watch them. And watching a lizard is very unrewarding because they will come out and bask, sit by a bush,

00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:32.840
and if they see you there then they'll decide they're not going to do very much for the rest of the day.

00:52:32.840 --> 00:52:41.840
To find out just what sleepy lizards get up to when no one's around, Mike's team use a rather bizarre device they call a waddle-ometer.

00:52:41.840 --> 00:52:49.840
It may look a little odd, but it records a lizard's GPS coordinates, counts its steps, and even notes whether it's in sun or shade,

00:52:49.840 --> 00:52:53.840
all without troubling the lizard and without anyone having to be there.

00:52:53.840 --> 00:53:04.840
So you think that's probably the secret world of the lizard, which no human being has ever seen, because if a human being is there the lizard won't behave that way?

00:53:04.840 --> 00:53:11.840
I'm sure that's part of it. You know the uncertainty principle, the closer you get to watch something the less normally it's behaving.

00:53:11.840 --> 00:53:21.840
And so it's only by getting these remote and new technologies that allow us to really get into the secret world of the lizards that we can find these really amazing things that they're doing.

00:53:21.840 --> 00:53:24.840
How extraordinary.

00:53:24.840 --> 00:53:38.840
One of their latest techniques uses miniature cameras which they use to study a very special lizard that we were also particularly keen to film.

00:53:38.840 --> 00:53:50.840
It's so rare that it was thought to be extinct for over 30 years, until it was thrust back into the public eye when it was discovered in some very unusual circumstances.

00:53:50.840 --> 00:53:54.840
There was a group of biologists who were doing a standard biological survey.

00:53:54.840 --> 00:54:00.840
They were just coming back to town to pick up supplies, and just on the road they saw a dead brown snake.

00:54:00.840 --> 00:54:05.840
Now most people just wouldn't even look at it because they're so common around here, but these were dedicated biologists.

00:54:05.840 --> 00:54:18.840
They stopped and had a look at it, they noticed there was a bulge in it, they thought let's see what it's been eating, open it up, and there was this lizard that no one had seen for 30 years, the figmy blue tongue lizard.

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:23.840
How lovely. I dare say it wasn't all that lovely when they actually saw it.

00:54:23.840 --> 00:54:35.840
Miniature cameras have produced images that are slowly helping to build up a comprehensive picture of the life of these rare little creatures.

00:54:35.840 --> 00:54:46.840
Their burrows are more than just homes. They're also hiding places where they can wait in ambush for spiders and crickets, but they don't seem too keen on ants.

00:54:46.840 --> 00:54:56.840
They also serve as bolt holes when danger approaches.

00:54:56.840 --> 00:55:01.840
Despite all this work, Mike's team had never recorded their life underground.

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:18.840
So we were able to help with a little of our own technology and record the first ever pictures of a pygmy blue tongue family. Three babies alongside their mother in their little hole.

00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:25.840
But all this technology, ingenious though it is, is no substitute for years of dedicated observation.

00:55:25.840 --> 00:55:34.840
Mike's approach of simply driving for miles across the Australian outback is very fruitful and you'll see lots of other things as well as lizards.

00:55:34.840 --> 00:55:39.840
Up here is just a wonderful place to visit.

00:55:39.840 --> 00:55:53.840
Eastern Grave, beautiful, isn't it? You won't catch a lizard doing that.

00:55:53.840 --> 00:56:03.840
Oh look, there's a pair just down there.

00:56:03.840 --> 00:56:11.840
It turned out that Mike had spotted two old friends.

00:56:11.840 --> 00:56:21.840
Yes, here's the male and the female. This is 1172 and 3345. I think they've been together for about 10 years, this pair.

00:56:21.840 --> 00:56:25.840
Really? We've got some other pairs that have been together for over 20 years.

00:56:25.840 --> 00:56:30.840
They stay together during the springtime and they mate towards the end of the spring and then they separate.

00:56:30.840 --> 00:56:37.840
But the next year the same two lizards will find them back together again, usually in the same place along this road too.

00:56:37.840 --> 00:56:39.840
Aren't they terrific?

00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:46.840
They use their tongues to pick up chemical signals and you can see they're actually sensing each other at the moment.

00:56:46.840 --> 00:56:54.840
I think that's really very touching. I said it's a risky business.

00:56:54.840 --> 00:57:08.840
With obsessive dedication and ever-advancing technology, who knows what Mike and his team will uncover about the secret lives of sleepy lizards.

00:57:08.840 --> 00:57:18.840
Next week, join me for the story of the elegant but much misunderstood snakes.

00:57:38.840 --> 00:57:48.840
Thank you for watching.
